


:: day_3_1_kira
<<set $day = 3>>
I wake up smiling.

That's not   that's not a thing I do. I don't wake up smiling. I wake up staring at the ceiling crack with the expression of someone being slowly lowered into their own grave. I wake up to the alarm I've snoozed three times and the taste of stale Monster and the ambient dread of a person whose first conscious thought each morning is a number   viewer count, bank balance, days until rent, some metric of inadequacy that my brain serves up before I've even opened my eyes, like a concierge of suffering. Good morning, sir. Today's disappointment is already prepared.

But today I wake up smiling.

My phone is on the floor beside the mattress, screen-down, and I know before I flip it over that there are messages on it, because the last thing I did before falling asleep was send a red heart emoji to a girl who kissed me in a parked Honda Civic and the first thing my brain produces this morning isn't a number. It's a feeling. Warm, stupid, disproportionate to any rational assessment of my circumstances. The feeling of someone who was, for a few hours last night, not alone.

I flip the phone. 10:02 AM. Three messages from Kira, stacked like gifts.

<<kira "good morning disaster ☀️ hope you actually slept and didn't stay up till 4am googling penguin anatomy again">>

<<kira "also i mentioned teki no hana at dinner right? here's the album that got me obsessed. listen to track 4 first it's transcendent">>

A Spotify link follows. The album art is stark   black background, white Japanese calligraphy, a single red circle that looks like a drop of blood or a rising sun depending on your mood.

<<kira "also. look who's streaming for me today 😂">>

A photo. Her cat   a grey tabby named Packet (named after data packets, because she's Kira and of course her cat has a networking joke for a name)   sitting on her keyboard, one paw on the space bar, staring into the webcam with the flat, superior gaze of an animal that has never once questioned its own worth. The ring light casts a halo around his ears. The caption she added in the photo editor reads: "KiraByte ft. Packet   10K viewers incoming."

<img src="images/scenes/day_3/day_3_1.jpeg" alt="Packet the cat sitting on Kira's keyboard" style="width:100%; height:auto;">

I smile at all three messages. Each one, individually, produces a separate, distinct smile   the first warm and sleepy, the second interested and touched, the third an actual grin that I can feel stretching muscles in my face that have been dormant for months. My cheeks ache from it. Not figuratively. Actually, physically ache. The muscles required for genuine sustained smiling have atrophied from disuse and they're protesting this sudden demand on their services like employees recalled from a very long furlough.

I type back.

<<eli "morning. i slept. no penguin research. packet has better lighting than me. track 4 first, got it.">>

<<kira "you're up before noon?? who are you and what have you done with eli">>

<<eli "some girl kissed me in a car last night and it apparently cured my circadian rhythm">>

<<kira "some girl??? SOME GIRL???">>

<<eli "a very specific girl. with very specific glasses. who very specifically bumped said glasses into my nose">>

<<kira "the glasses thing was YOUR fault. you leaned wrong">>

<<eli "i've never been kissed in a car before. there's no training program for the lean">>

<<kira "excuses 😤">>

<<kira "...but also hi. i'm glad you slept. i'm glad you're smiling. you ARE smiling right? i can feel it through the phone">>

<<eli "i'm smiling">>

<<kira "good. you should do it more. it suits you">>

<<kira "okay i have yoga in 20min and then a stream at 1. text me after? i want to see you tonight if you're free">>

<<eli "i'm free">>

<<kira "yay!! i'll come get you around 6? we can watch something at my place. i'll make tea">>

<<eli "sounds good">>

<<kira "❤️">>

<<eli "❤️">>

The red heart. Sent without hesitation this time. No hovering thumb, no agonizing over whether it's too much. She sends red, I send red. That's the protocol now. The treaty has been signed. My thumb hits the emoji and the message sends and I feel it   a small, warm detonation in my chest, the feedback loop of reciprocated affection, a circuit that's been open for eighteen months finally closing.

[[Put phone away|day_3_2_kira]]

:: day_3_2_kira
<<set $hint to "Think of and soltion">>
I put the phone down. Stare at the ceiling. The crack is still there   still growing, still splitting the plaster from kitchenette to pillow   but it looks different this morning. Less like a fault line. More like a map. A line on a surface that leads somewhere, even if I don't know where.

I get up.

The apartment is the same. The mattress, the desk, the fridge, the burrito wrapper I STILL haven't thrown away. But I move through it differently today   faster, lighter, with the residual energy of someone who has a plan for the evening that involves another human being who wants to be near them. I shower (hot water this time   I wait the full eight minutes, a luxury I'm suddenly willing to invest in). I use the Marriott shampoo again, because it worked yesterday and Kira noticed and the cause-and-effect chain of shampoo → Kira noticing → Kira kissing me is now a sacred algorithm I will never disrupt. I brush my teeth. I put on the grey shirt because Marcus said the grey shirt and Marcus was right about everything.

10:45 AM. I sit at the desk. The monitors wake. The dashboard from two days ago   the morning stream, the $3.50   is still visible on the second screen. I close it. Open a browser. Open Kira's stream page   not to watch (she hasn't gone live yet) but out of habit, the way you check the weather or the time, a reflexive verification that the world is still turning.

Her channel page loads. The banner is clean   KiraByte in pink and black, her mascot character, a schedule graphic that shows her stream times in a tidy grid. Beneath it, the stats that anyone can see: 12,400 followers. 890 average viewers. Partner status. A bio that reads: "games, vibes, and chaos. she/her. 21. 🎮💕"

12,400 followers. I have 54.

I don't look at the number with jealousy this time. Not exactly. The jealousy is there   it's always there, a low hum beneath everything, the baseline frequency of a person watching someone else succeed at the thing they're failing at   but it's mixed with something new. Something from yesterday. The conversation in her apartment. The dashboard. The $487. The truth she told me about faces and algorithms and the specific architecture of an economy that rewards her existence and ignores mine.

"If I were a guy with the exact same personality, the exact same humor, the exact same stream quality   I'd be you, Eli."

I hear her voice saying it. I hear it clearly, perfectly, the way you hear something that imprinted itself on your brain through sheer force of importance. She said it sitting in her desk chair, three feet from me, with the ring light off and the afternoon light catching her glasses, and she said it because she's honest and because she cares and because the truth is the truth whether it's comfortable or not.

The truth is: the system rewards a specific thing. Female. Cute. On camera. Those three inputs, in that combination, produce an output that I   male, average, poorly lit   cannot replicate.

Cannot replicate as myself.

The thought arrives like a knock at a door I didn't know existed. Quiet. Polite. Inevitable. I'm sitting at my desk in a grey shirt with shampoo'd hair and a girlfriend and $47 in the bank, and the thought knocks and I open the door and what's on the other side is not a plan, not yet, but the raw material for one.

What if the input changed?

Not me. Not my skill, my humor, my content. Those are fixed   those are the things Kira said are good, the things she said would work if the system weren't rigged. The variable isn't the content. The variable is the container. The voice. The name. The gender the algorithm reads when it decides who to surface and who to bury.

I open a new browser tab. I type, slowly, deliberately, each keystroke a step further from the person I was yesterday:

"voice changer software for streaming"

[[Research voice modulation software|day_3_3_kira]]

:: day_3_3_kira
<<set $hint to "Research the solution">>

I click the first result.

A guy in his mid-twenties sits in front of a decent setup and explains how real-time voice modulation works. The software is called VoxMorph. Free tier available. He demonstrates: his normal voice   deep, male, unremarkable   goes through the software and comes out the other side transformed. Higher. Softer. Breathy. Feminine. Not perfect, not indistinguishable, but close. Close enough for a stream where the face isn't visible. Close enough for voice-only.

<<chat "youtube_voice_guy" "The key is the formant shift. You're not just raising the pitch   that sounds robotic. You're reshaping the resonance. The formant controls how the voice 'fills' the space. Female voices have higher formants naturally, so you're simulating that acoustic shape.">>

I watch the whole video. Then I watch another. Then a third. By the fourth, I've opened VoxMorph's website in a separate tab and I'm reading the feature list with the focus of someone studying a blueprint for a building they intend to live in.

Real-time voice processing. Adjustable pitch, formant, breathiness, resonance. Low latency. OBS plugin available. Free tier supports basic modulation. Premium ($8/month) adds natural variation algorithms, background noise cancellation, and "vocal personality profiles"   presets designed to sound like specific types of voices. The preset list includes:

- Sweet/Breathy (labeled "Soft Girl")
- Husky/Low-Feminine (labeled "Velvet")
- Bright/Energetic (labeled "Sunshine")
- ASMR Whisper (labeled "Midnight")

I scroll to the free tier. Basic pitch and formant adjustment. No presets. No natural variation. But functional.

My hands are on the keyboard. My pulse is elevated. I can feel it in my wrists, in my neck, in the hinge of my jaw where I'm clenching without realizing it.

What am I doing?

The question floats through my brain like a subtitle on a foreign film   present, visible, and completely disconnected from the action on screen. Because the action continues regardless. My hands move. I download VoxMorph. The installer runs. The progress bar fills   10%, 30%, 60%, 100%. The software opens.

The research takes ninety minutes.

I approach it the way Kira approaches her stream strategy   methodically, analytically, the way a person with a ring light and cable couture approaches a problem. Not with the midnight desperation of a man grasping at anything   with the morning clarity of a man who slept well because someone kissed him and who is now, rested and caffeinated (I made coffee, actual coffee, with the hot plate and the cheap grounds I bought three weeks ago and never opened), applying his girlfriend's market analysis to a problem she identified.

Real-time voice modulation   pitch shift, formant adjustment, breathiness control. OBS plugin for direct stream integration. I watch four YouTube tutorials. I read the documentation. I download it. Install it. Open it.

The interface is clean   sliders, dials, a waveform visualizer, a test button. I pull the budget mic toward me. Clear my throat.

<<eli "Testing. One, two, three.">>

My voice, through the speakers. Male. Flat. The voice of EliPlays. The voice that earned $3.50 in two hours.

I move the sliders. Pitch up. Formant up. Breathiness to 38%.

<<eli "Testing. One, two, three.">>

The voice that returns is not mine.

It's female. Soft. A little breathy, a little uncertain, with a warmth in the lower register that sounds real   not robotic, not obviously processed, but like a woman in her early twenties speaking into a microphone for the first time. There's a shimmer at the edges where the algorithm meets the organic, a faint artificiality that a sound engineer would clock but a Twitch chat scrolling at 100 messages a minute would never notice.

I adjust. Pitch down slightly   too high sounds cartoonish. Formant down a hair. Breathiness to 35%. I test again.

<<eli "Hey everyone, welcome to the stream.">>

Better. The voice settles into a range that feels lived-in, like it belongs to a person who exists. A girl. A girl named 

I open Twitch. Account creation. The username field blinks.

[[Create a Twitch account|day_3_4_kira]]

:: day_3_4_kira
<<set $hint to "Create the new account">>
I type: ElliePlayz

Ellie. Two letters from Eli. A feminization so thin it's transparent, a costume made of gauze, and yet the moment I type it and hit enter and the account confirms, something shifts. ElliePlayz exists now. She has a username and an empty channel page and zero followers and she is, in every measurable way, exactly as much of a failure as EliPlays. But she has one thing he doesn't: the right input for the system.

I build the stream layout. No webcam   not yet, not ever, maybe. A background image: anime-style gradient, pink to purple, stars, borrowed from a free wallpaper site. The game capture. A text overlay in a cursive font   "ElliePlayz ✨"   positioned in the upper left. Chat on the right. The VoxMorph plugin routed through OBS, processing my microphone in real time.

It takes twenty minutes. The setup is crude   nothing like Kira's polished, professional layout with its branded overlays and custom emotes and animated alerts. This is a first draft. A sketch on a napkin. But it's functional. It works. The voice goes in male and comes out female and the screen shows a pink gradient and a name that belongs to no one and I am, as of 12:15 PM on a Thursday in January, ready to lie to the internet.

I think about Kira.

I think about her sitting in her desk chair yesterday, glasses off, cleaning them on her turtleneck, telling me the truth about the system. I think about the red heart she sent this morning. I think about her coming to pick me up at 6. I think about the fact that I am about to do something I cannot tell her about.

The thought produces a specific feeling   a small, sharp point of pressure behind my sternum, like a splinter driven just deep enough to be uncomfortable but not deep enough to demand attention. Guilt. Not the dramatic, crushing kind. The small kind. The kind you can work around, the kind you can reason with, the kind that fits in the corner of a room if you don't look at it directly.

It's just an experiment. I'm testing Kira's theory. She said the system rewards a specific input   I'm testing whether she's right. It's data collection. It's market research. I'll tell her about it when there's something to tell. When I have results. When the experiment means something beyond desperation.

The rationalizations stack neatly, like bricks. Each one plausible. Each one load-bearing. Together, they form a wall between what I'm doing and what it means, and I lean against the wall and it holds and I click "Go Live."

[[Go Live|day_3_5_kira]]

:: day_3_5_kira
