[[Keep playing|day_3_11_kira]]

:: day_3_11_kira
<<nobr>>
<<set $day_step to 11>>
<<set $viewers to 89>>
<<set $followers to 51>>
<<set $donations to 47>>
<<set $money += 27>>
<</nobr>>
Viewers: 72

The stream settles into a rhythm. I talk. They listen. I die in the game. They comfort me. I clutch a round. They explode. The dynamic is identical to what I experienced on Kira's stream yesterday   the warmth, the engagement, the feeling of being seen   except here it's directed at me. At Ellie. At the voice that is me and isn't me, the name that's mine and isn't mine.

The hour mark hits. I check the dashboard:

Duration: 1 hour 4 minutes
Peak Viewers: 89
Average Viewers: 63
New Followers: 51
Donations: $47.00
Chat Messages: 387

Forty-seven dollars.

The number again. The same number as my bank account. The same cosmic rhyme that the universe keeps writing, as if trying to tell me something   $47 to survive, $47 to thrive, the same figure on both sides of the equation, waiting for me to solve for x.

I stare at the dashboard. At the numbers. At the chat, still scrolling, still active, people still talking even during the pause between matches. 89 peak viewers. Fifty-one new followers. In one hour. As Eli, I gained zero followers yesterday and three-fifty in donations. As Ellie, I gained fifty-one followers and forty-seven dollars.

Same person. Same chair. Same desk. Same game. Same skill. Different voice. Different name.

Kira was right. About everything. The system rewards a specific input and I just fed it the correct one and it opened like a door that was never locked   just designed for a key I didn't have and now I have the key. It's made of software and lies, but it works.

<<chat "ElliePlayz" "Okay everyone, I think I'm going to wrap up for today! This was SO amazing, like genuinely I cannot believe this happened. I'm going to stream again tonight, same kind of thing, maybe a little later? You guys are the absolute best. Thank you thank thank you. Love you all!">>

<<chat "stream_viewer_a2" "noooo don't go 😭">>

<<chat "ChillVibesOnly" "best new streamer on twitch mark my words">>

<<chat "MidnightWhale99" "tonight. i'll be there.">>

<<chat "stream_viewer_a13" "followed and notis on! see you tonight ellie!! ❤️">>

[[End Stream|day_3_12_kira]]

:: day_3_12_kira
<<nobr>>
<<set $right_sidebar_mode to 0>>
<</nobr>>
I hit End Stream. VoxMorph stops processing. The monitoring feed goes quiet and the room fills with the sound of my real breathing   low, male, the sound that doesn't earn $47 in an hour.

I sit in the silence of my apartment. The fridge hums. The ceiling crack watches.

$47. Fifty-one followers. Eighty-nine peak viewers.

I pick up my phone. I want to tell someone. I want to tell Kira   you were right, the system is hackable, I hacked it, look at the numbers   but the telling would require explaining the hack, and the hack is a voice modulator and a fake name and a lie told to 89 people who think they were watching a girl, and explaining that to my girlfriend of two days feels like detonating the only good thing that's happened to me since I moved into this apartment.

I open Marcus's text thread instead.

[[Ask Marcus|day_3_13_kira]]

:: day_3_13_kira
<<set $hint to "Chat with Marcus">>
<<eli "hypothetical question">>

<<marc "oh no">>

<<eli "if someone made a second streaming account with a different persona would that be weird">>

<<marc "define different persona">>

<<eli "like a different name and voice and... vibe">>

<<marc "are you doing a bit or is this a real question">>

<<eli "real question">>

<<marc "depends. what kind of persona?">>

I stare at the phone. Delete the conversation in my head. I can't tell Marcus either. Marcus would ask questions. Marcus would connect dots. Marcus would say something like "so you're pretending to be a girl" in his flat, direct way that would make the truth sound exactly as absurd as it is.

<<eli "never mind. hypothetical over">>

<<marc "bro">>

<<marc "you know i can see you typing and deleting right">>

<<eli "i'm not typing and deleting">>

<<marc "you just typed and deleted that">>

<<eli "forget it. it was nothing. how's your day">>

<<marc "my day is fine. your day is suspicious. but okay">>

I put the phone down. Open the browser. Clear the VoxMorph tabs. Close OBS. The ElliePlayz dashboard remains on the second monitor   $47, 51 followers, 89 peak   and I let it sit there because the numbers feel like proof of something and I'm not ready to close the evidence.

It's 1:30 PM. Kira's stream started at 1. I open Twitch. Navigate to KiraByte. She's live   940 viewers, climbing, the familiar pink-and-black layout, the ring light, the bob, the bangs, the glasses. She's playing something casual, talking to chat, being Kira. Being the thing the system rewards. Being the thing I'm pretending to be.

I watch for ten minutes. She laughs at something a viewer says   the real laugh, the crinkle-nose one   and I feel two things simultaneously: the warm pulse of affection that her laugh always produces in me, and the cold click of understanding that her laugh, her face, her gender, is worth $487 in four hours and my face is worth $3.50 in two.

I close her stream. I can't watch right now. The math is too loud.

[[Think about possibilities|day_3_14_kira]]

:: day_3_14_kira
<<set $hint to "Think about the possibilities">>
<<set $day_step to 14>>
The afternoon opens up, wide and empty. Kira's streaming until 5. She's picking me up at 6. Four and a half hours with nothing to do but think, and thinking is the worst possible activity for a person who just committed his first act of profitable deception.
<<set $day = 3>>
I should eat. I open the freezer. Three microwave burritos left. I heat one   rotate at one minute, eat standing up, the same ritual   and wash it down with the last Monster in the fridge. The taste is familiar in a way that feels like a handshake from my old life, the life before the red hearts and the voice modulator, the life that was uniformly terrible instead of confusingly split between two kinds of hope.

I sit at the desk. The ElliePlayz dashboard glows on the second monitor. I open the browser.

The thought that arrives is not a thought I planned. It surfaces the way certain thoughts do   from below, from the place where calculations run in the background while the conscious mind is busy with other things. The thought is:

What if there was a face?

Not my face. A different face. A face that matched the voice.

I type: "how to look feminine fast"

The rabbit hole opens.

It starts with makeup tutorials. Dozens of them. YouTube is overflowing with videos titled "FEMININE MAKEUP FOR BEGINNERS" and "MTF MAKEUP TRANSFORMATION" and "How to Feminize Your Face in 10 Minutes." I watch three of them in succession   women and trans women and drag queens demonstrating contouring, highlighting, the strategic application of color to reshape the way light hits bone. The techniques are impressive but impractical for my purposes: makeup is visible on camera, especially on a webcam with bad resolution, and the time investment is significant. Twenty minutes of contouring before every stream, plus the risk of smudging, sweating, the makeup shifting mid-session.

I want something more permanent. Something that doesn't wash off.

There's the obvious solution. The one any tech-literate person in 2027 would think of first.

AI face-swap. Real-time deepfake filters. The technology exists   apps that map a generated face onto yours live, rendering a completely synthetic person over your webcam feed. Half the internet runs on some version of it.

I consider it for exactly twelve minutes.

The problem isn't quality   for pre-recorded content, AI faces are nearly flawless. A YouTube video, a TikTok, an edited clip   you could run a generated face through post-processing and nobody would ever know. But I'm not making videos. I'm live streaming. Real-time. Two hours of continuous webcam, every frame rendered on the fly, every expression tracked and mapped and composited at 30fps with zero room for error and the errors come. They always come.

There was a streamer   IndigoVeil   who ran a real-time AI face filter for four months. Premium GPU, calibrated lighting, the best software money could buy. It worked beautifully. Until she sneezed. One involuntary full-body sneeze that the motion-tracking couldn't follow. The AI lost the face for less than a second. One frame of the real face underneath   wrong jaw, wrong skin, wrong everything. One viewer screenshotted it. Reddit post. Viral in six hours. Clip channels dissecting it frame by frame. 2,000 viewers became zero overnight. Account deleted within 48 hours.

That's the premium version. I have a 2014 webcam and a thrift-store desk lamp. The composite would bleed at the hairline within minutes. The lip sync would lag on every third sentence. The micro-expressions   the wide-eyed gasp, the slow blink, the thinking face   would arrive 40 milliseconds behind the voice, and the regulars who watch every stream would clock it in a week.

Pre-made content? AI is perfect for it. Live streaming? It's a time bomb with a sneeze for a trigger and when it detonates   one screenshot, one Reddit post, one clip channel   there's no recovery. The internet doesn't forgive fake faces. It archives them.

I close the FaceMorph tab. AI is the last resort. The nuclear option I'd only touch if nothing else worked, and even then I'd be building on sand.

The voice modulator is a different story. VoxMorph works   it works well enough for now. The pitch shift is clean, the formant adjustment is natural, and through Twitch compression at 128kbps audio nobody can tell the difference between processed and organic. It's reliable in a way that AI face-swap isn't. A voice doesn't sneeze. A voice doesn't glitch at the hairline. The processing is consistent, frame after frame, hour after hour.

But "well enough for now" isn't "permanent." I can hear the seams if I listen for them   a faint digital shimmer on certain vowels, a flatness in the laugh that a real feminine voice wouldn't have, the way the software rounds off the edges of my natural register instead of truly reshaping them. Chat doesn't notice. Chat is listening through laptop speakers and earbuds and the general audio soup of a Twitch stream. But someone with good headphones and an ear for audio? A sound engineer? A voice coach? They'd hear the processing. They'd hear the machine inside the girl.

There's the fatigue. Two hours of speaking through VoxMorph strains my throat differently than normal talking   I'm pitching my natural voice into a range that the software can work with, staying in a narrow band that processes cleanly, avoiding the low register that makes the algorithm stutter. By the end of a long session my vocal cords ache in a way that feels unsustainable. I can't do this for years. The software is a bridge, not a destination.

RegenaForm's menu listed voice modulation   organic, not software. Tier 2. Actual restructuring of the vocal cords and resonance chambers. A voice that's mine, that's feminine, that doesn't need a plugin. That doesn't fatigue. That doesn't shimmer on certain vowels.

But that's later. That's Tier 2. That's thousands of Dollars I don't have yet.

For now, VoxMorph holds. The voice is the most reliable part of the illusion   more reliable than the crop-line framing, more reliable than the wig, infinitely more reliable than any AI face would be. It's enough. For now.

The face is the problem. The face needs to be real.

I need something real. Something that IS the face, not something projected onto it. Something a viewer can screenshot and frame-by-frame and zoom into and find nothing wrong   because the lashes are actual lashes and the brows are actual brows and every cell is mine.

The search mutates. "How to look feminine without makeup." "Facial feminization non-surgical." "Subtle feminization techniques for streaming." The results shift from YouTube to forums   Reddit threads, niche communities, spaces where people discuss passing and presentation and the specific art of looking like someone other than who you were born as.

I read for forty-five minutes. The information is dense, detailed, and organized by the kind of people who have spent years thinking about exactly this problem. Brow shaping. Eyelash extensions. Skincare routines that change the texture and luminosity of skin. Hair   real hair, wig options, styling techniques. Each topic is a door, and behind each door is a room full of more doors, and the architecture of the rabbit hole is designed to keep you descending and then   on the second page of a Google search for "facial feminization fast results"   the ad.

It doesn't look like an ad. It looks like a search result, clean and authoritative, with a thumbnail image of a chrome double helix and text that reads:

RegenaForm   Unlock Your True Form DNA-based bioengineering. No scars. Full sensory integration. Consultations available.

I click it.

The website loads and it's   beautiful. In the way that a luxury car showroom is beautiful, or a five-star hotel lobby. The design communicates money and competence and the specific brand of confidence that comes from knowing your product works. White space. Clean typography. The chrome double-helix logo rotating slowly in the header. A tagline beneath it: "We don't change you. We unlock you."

I scroll. Before-and-after gallery. Each transformation shown in high resolution, the images so detailed they look medical and so beautiful they look editorial. Faces shifting from one configuration to another   masculine to feminine, angular to soft, forgettable to magnetic. The changes range from subtle (a jawline refined, skin cleared) to dramatic (full facial feminization, the bone structure literally reshaped, a different person emerging from beneath the same skin).

Services. Listed in tiers. Tier 1: Facial Feminization. Eyelashes, brow sculpting, nose refinement, jawline contouring, lip enhancement, skin treatments. Tier 2: Voice Modulation (organic   not software). Tier 3: Hair. Tier 4: Body contour. Tiers 5 through 8: escalating, invasive, transformative. The prices are in Dollars, a cryptocurrency I've seen in niche forums. The conversion rate to USD is roughly 1:1.

Tier 1 starts at 500 $. Individual features are cheaper. Eyelash enhancement: 200-350 $. Brow sculpting: 300-500 $. The numbers are large for someone with $47 in the bank but small for someone who just earned $47 in an hour.

I read the FAQ.

"Is the process reversible?"   "All modifications can be revised or reversed. See our Revision Policy."

"Does it hurt?"   "Our DNA-based technology integrates with your own genetic material. Most clients describe the sensation as 'warm' or 'tingling.' No anesthesia required for Tier 1."

"How long does it take?"   "Tier 1 procedures range from 20 minutes to 2 hours. Results are immediate."

Immediate. No surgery. No scarring. No downtime. I read the word "immediate" three times and each time it sounds more like a promise and less like a claim.

At the bottom of the FAQ, a button: Book Free Consultation.

I click it before the rational part of my brain can file an objection. The form loads. Name: Ellie. Email: ellieplayz.stream@gmail.com (created on the spot, fingers moving faster than thought). Phone: my real number. Date: today. Time: the dropdown shows 2:30 PM available.

I submit. Confirmation page: "Thank you, Ellie! Your consultation with Dr. Voss is confirmed for 2:30 PM today. 1440 Meridian Ave, Suite 12."

1440 Meridian. Four blocks from my apartment. The clinic that promises to unlock my "true form" is a fifteen-minute walk from the building where I've been slowly decomposing for eight months. The proximity feels like fate or marketing. Probably marketing.

I close the browser. Open it. Close it. Open it one more time, look at the confirmation, and close it for real.

I check the time. 2:14 PM. Forty-six minutes until the consultation.

I put on the hoodie. The sneakers. I look at myself in the phone's front camera   the hair (still good from the Marriott shampoo), the grey shirt, the face. The face that earned $3.50. The face that is, in approximately forty-five minutes, going to sit in a clinic and ask how to become a different face.

<<link "Go to the consultation">><<set $hint to "Go to the consultation">><<goto neighbourhood>><</link>>

:: day_3_15_kira

The clinic is not what I expected.

I expected sterile. I expected fluorescent lights and linoleum floors and the antiseptic smell of a dentist's waiting room   the aesthetic of medical discomfort, the visual language of "this will hurt but it's necessary." What I get instead is a door at the end of a hallway on the second floor of a building that looks, from the outside, like every other building on Meridian   brick, unremarkable, the kind of facade that says "accountant's office" or "insurance broker" or "nothing to see here."

Suite 12. A frosted glass door with the RegenaForm logo etched into it   the double helix in chrome, elegant, catching the hallway light like a piece of jewelry. No signage beyond the logo. No description of services. No "ENTER HERE TO HAVE YOUR FACE RESTRUCTURED." Just the helix and the suite number and the quiet confidence of a business that doesn't need to advertise because the people who need it will find it.

I push the door open.

The waiting area is small and immaculate. Hardwood floors, warm-toned, polished to a shine that makes the apartment's hardwood look like plywood. Two low couches in dove grey, the kind with precise right angles and cushions that look like they've never been sat on. A glass coffee table with a single orchid in a white ceramic pot and a stack of magazines that are not magazines   they're lookbooks, glossy portfolios of RegenaForm's work, each page a before-and-after that blurs the line between medicine and art. The lighting is recessed, warm, the kind that makes skin look good regardless of what the skin actually looks like. A faint ambient sound   not music, not quite white noise, but something between the two, a frequency that says relax on a level your brain processes without your permission and behind the reception desk a girl.

I see her and my brain does something it rarely does   it stops. Full halt. No internal monologue, no anxious chatter, no running calculation of my inadequacies. Just: stop. Look. Process.

She's beautiful. Not in the way Kira is beautiful   Kira's beauty is specific, angular, made of sharp bobs and wire frames and the intelligence behind her eyes. Lina's beauty is... universal. Impossible to categorize. She could be any ethnicity, all of them, none of them   honey-toned skin that glows like it's generating its own light source, large doe-shaped eyes in an amber-gold that I've never seen in a human face outside of colored contacts, soft wavy hair in caramel and brown past her shoulders, full lips in a smile that's warm and practiced and slightly, almost imperceptibly, too perfect. She's wearing a fitted dove-grey dress with the chrome double-helix embroidered on the chest, and everything about her reads as "advertisement." Not in a cheap way. In the way that a luxury car in a showroom window is an advertisement   the product is its own pitch.

<<lina "Welcome to RegenaForm! You must be Ellie.">>

Her voice matches. Melodic. Modulated. Every syllable placed like a note in a composition.

<<eli "Uh. Yeah. Hi.">>

<<lina "Wonderful. Dr. Voss is expecting you. Can I get you anything before your consultation? Water, tea, sparkling?">>

<<eli "I'm fine. Thanks.">>

<<lina "Of course. Please have a seat   the doctor will be right with you.">>

I sit on the grey couch. It's as uncomfortable as it looks   beautiful but rigid, designed for aesthetics rather than human bodies. I pick up one of the lookbooks. The pages are heavy stock, matte finish, each spread showing a transformation: the left page a "before" shot, the right an "after." The changes range from subtle   a jawline softened, a nose refined, skin cleared   to dramatic. Full facial feminization. Body contour. Results that look less like surgery and more like a person being translated into a different language of themselves.

I'm looking at a spread showing a jaw transformation   angular-masculine to soft-feminine, the bone structure literally reshaped   when a door opens at the back of the waiting area and a person steps through.

[[Doctor arrives|day_3_16_kira]]

:: day_3_16_kira
<<set $hint to "Meet Doctor Maren Voss">>
<<set $day_step to 16>>
Dr. Maren Voss.

They're   and I use "they" because my brain can't settle on a pronoun and neither, it seems, can Dr. Voss's face   tall, lean, somewhere in their mid-thirties, with the kind of bone structure that exists in the exact center of a spectrum that most people live at the edges of. Sharp cheekbones but soft mouth. Strong brow ridge but arched, feminine eyebrows. Ash-blonde hair pulled back sleekly from a face that is striking precisely because it refuses to be categorized. They're wearing a tailored white lab coat over a black high-collared shirt, slim black trousers, minimalist white shoes. A thin silver bracelet on the left wrist. The RegenaForm helix pin on the lapel, catching light.

Their eyes are cool grey-green. Steady. The kind of eyes that don't look at you   they read you. Like a barcode scanner in a face.

<<dr_maren "Ellie. I'm Dr. Maren Voss. Welcome.">>

The voice is measured, calm, with an accent I can't place   something European, maybe Scandinavian, smoothed by years of professional neutrality into a register that gives nothing away.

<<eli "Hi. Thanks for fitting me in today.">>

<<dr_maren "Of course. Shall we?">>

They gesture toward the door they emerged from. I follow them through.

[[Go to the consultation room|day_3_17_kira]]

:: day_3_17_kira
<<set $hint to "The consultation">>
<<set $day_step to 17>>
The consultation room is a continuation of the waiting area's aesthetic   warm lighting, wood and glass, the clinical minimized in favor of the comfortable. There's no examination table. Instead, two chairs facing each other across a low table, with a large screen mounted on the wall behind Dr. Maren's chair. It looks more like a design studio than a medical office.

I sit. Dr. Maren sits across from me. They cross one leg over the other   an elegant, controlled motion   and fold their hands in their lap. The grey-green eyes settle on my face with the attentiveness of someone cataloging its features.

<<dr_maren "So. Tell me what brings you here.">>

I've rehearsed this. On the walk over, in the elevator, in the waiting room. I've assembled a script that sounds reasonable, practical, the kind of thing a person might say without sounding like a person who's pretending to be a woman on the internet for money.

<<eli "I'm a streamer. Online content creator. I'm looking to... upgrade my appearance for camera. Just   small things. I want to look a little more polished. Maybe a bit more... feminine? Not   not like a full transformation. Just camera-friendly.">>

I hear myself say it and it sounds almost normal. Almost like a reasonable request from a person who works on camera and wants to look better. Dr. Maren's expression doesn't change   the same steady, neutral attention, the barcode scanner reading.

<<dr_maren "I see. Can you be more specific about what you'd like to change?">>

<<eli "I'm not sure, honestly. I don't know what's possible. I just know that my face... isn't doing what I need it to do.">>

<<dr_maren "What do you need it to do?">>

<<eli "...make people stop scrolling.">>

Something shifts in Dr. Maren's expression. Not a smile   they don't smile, exactly   but a softening. A recognition. As if "make people stop scrolling" is a diagnosis they've heard before, a presenting symptom they know how to treat.

<<dr_maren "May I?">>

They gesture toward my face. I nod, unsure what I'm agreeing to. They lean forward and   with the precise, impersonal care of a jeweler examining a stone   they turn my head gently with two fingers on my chin. Left. Right. Center. Their touch is cool and dry and completely devoid of warmth or hesitation. Professional in the truest sense: my face is an object they're assessing, not a person they're touching.

<<dr_maren "Good bone structure beneath the surface. The jawline has potential for significant feminization with minimal intervention. The brow ridge is low   that's actually advantageous. Your skin responds well to treatment. Eyes are proportionally balanced. Nose would benefit from refinement but isn't a priority.">>

They're describing my face the way an architect describes a building they intend to renovate   noting the foundation, identifying the load-bearing walls, planning what to preserve and what to strip away.

<<dr_maren "Let me show you something.">>

They turn to the wall-mounted screen. A few taps on a tablet and my face appears   captured by a high-resolution camera I didn't notice built into the frame of the screen. The image is sharp, mercilessly detailed. Every pore, every blemish, every asymmetry. My face at a resolution I've never seen it in, exposed under clinical lighting, and looking at it is like hearing a recording of my own voice   familiar but wrong, the same and not the same, the private made public.

Dr. Maren touches the screen. A digital overlay appears on my face   lines and contours, like an architectural blueprint, mapping the bone structure beneath the skin.

<<dr_maren "Here's what I'm seeing.">>

They draw with their finger. Lines appear.

<<dr_maren "The masculine markers are concentrated here   the brow ridge, the jaw angle, the nose width. These are the features that code 'male' to the viewer's eye, usually within the first half-second of seeing a face. Everything else   your eye shape, your lip proportions, your cheekbone placement   is actually quite neutral. You're closer to the center of the spectrum than you think.">>

<<eli "Closer to the center of what spectrum?">>

<<dr_maren "The gender presentation spectrum. Your face reads male because of four or five specific features. Remove or soften those, and the rest is... ambiguous. Open to interpretation. Camera-friendly, as you put it.">>

They tap the screen again. The overlay shifts   the digital lines redraw, and my face changes. Not dramatically. Subtly. The jaw softens. The brow smooths. The lashes lengthen. The skin clears. The face on the screen is still recognizably mine but also recognizably different   like a relative I've never met, a cousin who inherited different genes from the same pool.

I stare at it.

<<dr_maren "This is achievable with Tier 1 modifications alone. Facial feminization. Eyelashes, brow sculpting, skin refinement. No structural bone work, no implants, nothing invasive. The procedures are DNA-based   we use your own genetic material, so there's no rejection risk, no scarring, full sensory preservation. Recovery time for each feature is six to twelve hours.">>

<<eli "And the cost?">>

<<dr_maren "Depends on the selections. The full Tier 1 menu ranges from 500 to 4,500 Dollars. Individual features are priced separately. We also offer... introductory packages.">>

They say "introductory packages" the way a dealer says "first one's free"   casually, offhandedly, as if the generosity is routine and not calculated.

<<dr_maren "For new clients, we offer a complimentary eyelash enhancement   our most popular introductory procedure   and for streamers specifically, a professional-grade wig from our cosmetics line. No charge. Consider it... a sample.">>

A sample. A taste. The thing that gets you through the door and seated in the chair, the thing that lets you see the results on your own face in your own mirror, the thing that makes the next tier not just possible but inevitable.

I know what this is. I can see the funnel   the marketing, the psychology, the escalation ladder. First visit free. First modification free. Then the brow sculpt for 300 $. Then the nose for 500. Then the jaw for 800. Each step a little more, each result a little better, each number on the dashboard a little higher, until you've climbed the ladder so far that looking down would mean seeing a person you no longer recognize as yourself.

I know what this is and I say yes anyway.

<<eli "What would you recommend? For... starting?">>

Dr. Maren's grey-green eyes meet mine. Steady. Reading.

<<dr_maren "The complimentary eyelash enhancement   Natural-Long variety. Dense, dark, naturally long. Subtle enough that most people won't identify the change consciously, but the effect on camera is significant. Lashes frame the eyes. Framed eyes hold attention. Attention is the currency you're trading in.">>

<<eli "And beyond that?">>

<<dr_maren "If you'd like to invest in one paid procedure today, I'd recommend the brow sculpt. Feathered   soft, natural-looking, a slight feminine arch. It opens the eye area, reduces the masculine coding of the brow ridge, and pairs beautifully with the lash enhancement. Three hundred Dollars.">>

300 $. Which converts to   I do the math   roughly $300 in real currency. Which I don't have. Which I absolutely don't have. My bank account has $47 plus the $3.50 from yesterday that hasn't cleared yet. I cannot afford a $300 cosmetic procedure. I cannot afford a $30 cosmetic procedure. I cannot afford the coffee Marcus bought me two days ago.

But.

ElliePlayz made $47 in one hour. One stream. If the second stream tonight does even half as well   and the numbers suggest it will, given the follower base and the algorithm momentum   that's another $25-30. Tomorrow, another stream. Next week, multiple streams. The math isn't theoretical. It's already proven. The system works. The voice works. The money is real.

I can pay for this. Not today. But soon. Very soon.

<<eli "Can I do a payment plan?">>

<<dr_maren "We offer credit through our internal system. Dollars-based. The eyelashes are complimentary today. The brow sculpt   300 $   can be billed to your streaming account with a seven-day payment window.">>

<<eli "My streaming account?">>

<<dr_maren "ElliePlayz, correct?">>

I freeze. I didn't   I didn't give them that name. The consultation form said "Ellie." Just Ellie. No channel name, no streaming details. But Dr. Maren says "ElliePlayz" like they've been watching, like they know, like the information arrived through a channel I can't see.

<<eli "How did you ">>

<<dr_maren "We keep our ear to the ground. New streamers with... potential... tend to find their way here. It's not unusual. Many of our most successful clients began exactly where you are.">>

They say it smoothly. Too smoothly. The words are a bridge over a question I should be asking   how do you know my stream name?   but the bridge is well-built and the far side has a brow sculpt and eyelashes waiting, and I cross it without looking down.

<<eli "Okay. Let's do the eyelashes and the brow sculpt.">>

<<dr_maren "Excellent and the wig   our gift to you. We have a range specifically designed for camera use. Natural appearance, adjustable fit, fiber that responds to lighting like real hair. Our coordinator will help you select one after the procedure.">>

They stand. Extend a hand. I shake it   their grip is firm, dry, the handshake of someone who shakes hands the way they do everything else: with precision and intent.

<<dr_maren "If you'll follow me.">>

[[Follow Dr. Maren to the procedure room|day_3_18_kira]]

:: day_3_18_kira
<<set $hint to "The procedure">>
<<set $day_step to 18>>
The procedure room is behind another frosted glass door, and it's the first space in the clinic that actually looks medical   a reclining chair that splits the difference between dental and spa, adjustable lighting rigs, a tray of instruments I can't identify, and a screen showing my face again, this time with the target areas highlighted. The eyelashes in blue. The brows in green.

I sit in the chair. It reclines smoothly   none of the hydraulic wheezing of my desk chair, just a silent, engineered glide backward until I'm staring at the ceiling, which is featureless and white and nothing like the crack in my apartment ceiling, which I find oddly disorienting. My ceilings should have cracks. That's the deal.

<<dr_maren "The eyelash procedure takes approximately twenty minutes. The brow sculpt, thirty. You'll experience mild warmth at the application sites   that's the bioactive compound integrating with your follicular DNA. No pain. No downtime. Results are immediate.">>

<<eli "Immediate?">>

<<dr_maren "The modifications grow from your own genetic template. We're not attaching anything   we're activating dormant potential in your existing cells. The lashes will be fully extended by the time you leave the chair. The brows will reshape over the course of two to three hours.">>

Activating dormant potential. I wonder if they use that phrase deliberately   if it's medical terminology or marketing copy, if there's a difference, if it matters. The idea that my cells contain the blueprint for feminine eyelashes, dormant, waiting to be switched on   it's either science or poetry and both are doing something to the inside of my chest.

They apply something to my eyelids. Cool, gel-like, with a faint chemical tang that doesn't smell medical but doesn't smell not medical. The warmth follows   gradual, spreading, not unpleasant. Like someone pressing warm fingertips against the skin around my eyes.

<<dr_maren "You can close your eyes. The process works best with the lids relaxed.">>

I close my eyes. The warmth deepens. I can feel something happening   not movement, not growth, but a stirring. A cellular-level activity that registers as a hum at the edge of perception, like hearing a sound just below the threshold of hearing. My lashes tingle. The skin around my eyes tightens slightly, then relaxes.

Twenty minutes. I lie there with my eyes closed and I think about Kira.

I think about her sitting in her desk chair yesterday, explaining click-through rates, the afternoon light on her glasses. I think about the pixel-heart tattoo beneath my palm when our fingers interlaced at the izakaya. I think about the kiss   the glasses bumping my nose, the seatbelt catching, the lip gloss. I think about the red heart she sent this morning and the one I sent back.

I think about the fact that I'm lying in a chair in a clinic four blocks from my apartment having my eyelashes modified by a doctor with no discernible gender, using money I don't have, earned by pretending to be a woman, hidden from the girlfriend I've had for approximately 36 hours.

The splinter in my chest pushes a little deeper.

<<dr_maren "Eyelashes complete. Opening your eyes now.">>

[[Open eyes after eyelash procedure|day_3_19_kira]]

:: day_3_19_kira
<<set $hint to "Open your eyes">>
I open them. The ceiling is still white. Still crack-free. Dr. Maren holds a mirror up   a real mirror, clinical-grade, the kind that shows everything.
<<set $appearance   to 3>>
<<set $femininity   to 1>>
<<set $playerImage = "images/user/tier_0_1.jpeg">>
<img src="images/user/tier_0_1.jpeg" alt="Eli's face after eyelash enhancement" class="scene-image">
My eyes look different.

The lashes are   they're mine. They look like they grew from my face, because they did, because that's how this technology works. But they're longer. Darker. Denser. A fringe of dark lashes that frames my eyes the way a frame holds a painting, creating a border that draws attention inward, toward the brown irises, toward the look in them, toward me. The effect is subtle and enormous simultaneously   if you weren't paying attention you'd miss it, but if you were looking at my face on a stream, on a webcam, in a thumbnail, your eyes would catch on the lashes the way eyes catch on movement. They're an anchor. A reason to look twice.

<<dr_maren "Natural-Long. Dense, dark, no maintenance required. They'll grow at the same rate as your natural lashes and can be trimmed if desired, though I wouldn't recommend it. The length is optimized for camera distance.">>

I stare at my reflection. The face is still mine. Still the pale skin, the dark circles, the acne, the wide-bridge nose, the thin lips. But the eyes are different. The eyes look   not feminine, not yet, but interesting. Like something changed in the equation and the result shifted, and the shift is small but visible, like a star moving in the sky.

<<eli "...wow.">>

<<dr_maren "Shall we proceed with the brows?">>

<<eli "Yeah. Yes.">>

The brow procedure is similar   a gel application, warmth, the cellular stirring. This time Dr. Maren shapes with their fingers, tracing the new arch with a precision that feels more like sculpture than medicine. They murmur coordinates to themselves   angles, millimeters, the geometry of a face being redrawn in real time.

<<dr_maren "Feathered. Soft, natural-looking. The arch opens the eye area and raises the apparent brow line, reducing the visual weight of the brow ridge. The effect is... lightening. The face becomes lighter. More open. More inviting.">>

Thirty minutes. The warmth fades. Dr. Maren holds the mirror up again.

[[See the new brows|day_3_20_kira]]

:: day_3_20_kira
<<set $hint to "See the new brows">>
The brows are   they're subtle. The change from my previous brows   thick, masculine, low-set, slightly unruly   to the Feathered sculpt is like the difference between a rough sketch and a clean line drawing. The new brows are thinner, shaped, with a soft feminine arch that lifts the eye area and creates the impression of more space between the eyes and the hairline. My forehead looks different. Taller. Smoother. The brow ridge, which Dr. Maren identified as a "masculine marker," is visually reduced   not gone, but softened, the way turning down the bass changes a song without removing any notes.

Combined with the lashes, the effect is 
<<set $appearance   to 4>>
<<set $femininity   to 2>>
<<set $playerImage = "images/user/tier_0_2.jpeg">>
<img src="images/user/tier_0_2.jpeg" alt="Eli's face after eyelash enhancement" class="scene-image">


I look at my face in the mirror and I feel a vertigo that has nothing to do with height or motion. The face is mine. I can trace every feature back to the face I've been avoiding in my bathroom mirror for eight months. But the sum is different from the parts. The lashes plus the brows plus the accumulated effect of two small changes equals something new   something that lives in the space between what I was and what I might become, and looking at it is like looking through a window into a room I didn't know my house contained.

<<dr_maren "The brows will continue to refine over the next two to three hours as the cells complete their restructuring. By this evening, the arch will be fully defined.">>

<<eli "It looks... I look...">>

<<dr_maren "You look like yourself. Just... more so.">>

They say it with the ghost of a smile   the first expression I've seen on their face that isn't clinical neutrality. It's the smile of an artist looking at a work in progress. Satisfaction. Potential recognized.

[[Wig fitting with Lina|day_3_21_kira]]

:: day_3_21_kira
<<set $hint to "Wig fitting with Lina">>
Lina handles the wig fitting. She leads me to a small side room   more boutique than clinic, with a lit vanity mirror and a wall of wig forms displaying a dozen styles, each one looking more like real hair than anything I've seen in a store.

<<lina "Dr. Voss mentioned you're streaming voice-only currently? Planning to add a camera element eventually?">>

<<eli "Maybe. I'm not sure yet.">>

<<lina "Well, when you are, this will help.">>

She pulls a wig from the wall   medium-length, brownish-auburn, soft waves, the kind of hair that looks like it belongs to a woman who uses conditioner and knows what a hair mask is. She sets it on the counter in front of me.

<<lina "This is our StreamLine series. Designed specifically for camera use   the fiber responds to light the way biological hair does, so it doesn't have that wig 'sheen' that cheap synthetics get under LED or ring light. Adjustable cap, invisible lace front, blends with your natural hairline.">>

She helps me put it on. The process is more involved than I expected   she tucks my real hair, adjusts the cap, aligns the lace front, uses some kind of adhesive along the edges. Her hands are efficient, impersonal, warm. She smells like vanilla and something clean, like freshly laundered linen.

<<lina "There.">>

[[Look in the mirror with the new wig|day_3_22_kira]]

:: day_3_22_kira
<<set $hint to "Look in the mirror with the new wig">>
<<set $loan        to 300>>
She turns me toward the mirror. I don't recognize myself.

<<set $playerImage = "images/user/tier_0_3.jpeg">>
<img src="images/user/tier_0_3.jpeg" alt="Eli's face after eyelash enhancement" class="scene-image">

That's not   that's not accurate. I recognize myself. The eyes are mine. The nose is mine. The mouth is mine. But the frame has changed. The lashes, the brows, the hair   three modifications, two of them free   and the face in the mirror looks like a cousin. A sister. A version of me from a parallel universe where the genetic dice rolled differently, where the X chromosome doubled, where the person I might have been got to exist instead of the person I am.

The wig falls in soft waves to my shoulders. The brownish-auburn color works with my skin tone   the pale complexion that looked sickly with my greasy brown hair looks delicate with this, like the pallor is intentional, porcelain rather than neglected. The lashes frame the eyes. The brows lift the face. The hair completes the composition.

I look androgynous. Not female   not yet, not with the jaw and the nose and the flat chest and the Adam's apple   but not definitively male either. I look like a question the viewer's brain has to answer, and the answer depends on context. In the right lighting, with the right angle, from the nose down or the eyes up 

From the nose up.

An idea forms. Not a full plan   a fragment, a shard, the edge of something. Camera from the nose down. The wig. The voice modulator. The lashes and the brows framing the visible portion of the face. Enough to suggest. Enough to imply. Enough to let the viewer's brain answer the question the way it wants to.

<<lina "The wig comes with a care kit   storage form, fiber shampoo, a styling guide. It's yours. A welcome gift from RegenaForm.">>

<<eli "Thank you.">>

<<lina "Of course. Dr. Voss wanted me to mention   your Dollars credit is active. The brow sculpt balance is due within seven days and if you'd like to explore Tier 1 further... the full menu is available through our app.">>

An app. Of course there's an app. Of course the bioengineering clinic that makes you prettier has a mobile application for browsing the catalog and scheduling procedures and managing your transformation like a subscription service. Of course.

<<lina "Of course. We'll see you soon, Ellie.">>

She says it with the certainty of a person who knows the difference between "goodbye" and "see you soon," who understands that the free eyelashes and the free wig are not charity but investment, and that the investment will pay off because it always does, because the door only opens one way.

[[Take of the wig and Leave Facility|day_3_23_kira]]

:: day_3_23_kira
<<set $hint to "Leave the clinic">>
<<set $playerImage = "images/user/tier_0_2.jpeg">>
<<set $day_step to 23>>
I leave the clinic with a bag containing the wig and the care kit and the receipt for the brow sculpt   300 $, seven-day payment window   and I walk four blocks back to my apartment in the January cold with my new lashes catching snowflakes I didn't notice starting and my new brows still subtly reshaping beneath the skin and the wig in a bag that swings from my hand like a prop from a life I'm rehearsing for.

The splinter in my chest is there. I can feel it   small, sharp, positioned in the exact place where the warm pulse of Kira's red heart lives. Two sensations occupying the same space: the guilt and the hope, the lie and the love, the secret and the girl who doesn't know it exists.

I check my phone. Two hours until Kira picks me up.

Two hours to hide everything.

<<link "Go Home">><<set $hint to "Go Home and Wait in Bedroom">><<goto neighbourhood>><</link>>

:: day_3_24_kira

I spend the two hours before Kira arrives in a state of controlled panic.

The wig is in the closet, buried beneath the pile of clothes on the floor where the rod fell four months ago. The care kit is shoved behind the mini-fridge. The RegenaForm receipt is deleted from my email and the browser history is cleared and the confirmation page is closed and every digital trace of the afternoon has been scrubbed with the thoroughness of a person who watches too many crime procedurals and not enough relationship advice videos.

The physical traces are harder to hide.

I stand in the bathroom. I look in the mirror. Not the phone camera   the actual mirror, the one I've been avoiding for eight months, the one that shows everything without filter or mercy.

The eyelashes are there. Long, dark, dense. They catch the light from the flickering overhead bulb and cast tiny shadows on my cheekbones that weren't there yesterday. The brows are reshaping   I can see it happening in real time, the slow migration from thick-masculine to feathered-feminine, the arch lifting by degrees, the hair refining itself from scraggly to sculpted. Dr. Maren said two to three hours. It's been ninety minutes. The transformation is halfway done.

Will she notice?

Of course she'll notice. Kira notices everything. She noticed the shampoo. She noticed when I changed my stream title two months ago. She noticed the brand of energy drink I switched to in September and asked if I was okay because "people only change energy drinks when something's wrong." She is an observation engine disguised as a girl with wire-frame glasses and she will look at my face and she will see the lashes and the brows and she will ask and I will lie.

The plan   "new skincare routine"   feels paper-thin, a tissue-paper excuse stretched over a truth-shaped hole. But it's what I have and it might work, because the changes are subtle, because the lashes look natural, because the brows are only halfway to their final shape, because Kira has never paid close attention to my brow line (probably   I don't actually know what Kira has and hasn't paid attention to, given that she apparently memorized my 3 AM voice and my thumbnail-picking habit from video calls).

I splash water on my face. Pat dry. Look in the mirror one more time.

The face looking back is mine. Different, but mine. The lashes make my eyes look bigger. The partially reshaped brows make my forehead look smoother. The combined effect is   what did Dr. Maren say?   lighter. The face is lighter. More open. Not feminine, not yet, but softer in a way that's hard to pin down, like someone adjusted the contrast settings on a photograph.

My phone buzzes.

<<kira "leaving in 10! i'm bringing snacks. you better have cleared a space on that couch">>

<<eli "i don't have a couch">>

<<kira "your mattress. you better have cleared a space on your mattress. actually no we're watching at my place. your apartment makes me sad">>

<<eli "it makes me sad too">>

<<kira "then it's settled. my place. Packet misses you">>

<<eli "i met Packet once and he hissed at me">>

<<kira "that's his love language 😤">>

I smile. The muscles ache less today. They're adapting.

[[Kira picks arrive|day_3_25_kira]]

:: day_3_25_kira
<<set $hint to "Go with Kira">>
<<set $locationImage to "images/locations/neighbourhood.jpeg">>
<<set $locationName to "Street">>
She picks me up at 6:07. Seven minutes late because she stopped to buy mochi from her mom's bakery ("Mom asked who I was bringing snacks for and I said 'a friend' and she gave me the look, Eli. THE LOOK. The Japanese-mother-who-knows-everything look.").

I get in the Civic. Cherry blossom. Heater. The warm, enclosed space that smells like her. She's wearing a cream cardigan over a black tank top, her hair freshly washed and slightly fluffier than usual, the bob bouncing at her jawline. The wire-frame glasses are slightly crooked   she pushes them up as I close the door.

<<kira "Hi.">>

<<eli "Hi.">>

She leans across the console. I lean to meet her. The kiss is quick   a hello kiss, a punctuation mark, a "this is a thing we do now" kiss   and her lips are warm and taste like the mochi she was sampling in the car. When she pulls back, she looks at my face.

She looks at my face for three seconds.

My heart rate spikes. Not the warm spike from yesterday's kiss   the cold spike, the adrenaline spike, the "she's going to see it" spike that sends electricity through my fingertips and makes my mouth go dry.

<<kira "Did you do something to your face?">>

There it is. The question. The one I prepared for, rehearsed, armed myself against. She asks it lightly   not accusatory, not suspicious, just curious, the tone of someone who notices a change in a face they've been studying for eighteen months.

<<eli "New skincare routine.">>

The lie exits my mouth with a smoothness that surprises me. Three words, evenly delivered, the vocal equivalent of a shrug. I even add a self-deprecating half-smile, the kind that says "I know, I know, I'm finally taking care of myself, don't make it a thing."

Kira squints. Her eyes narrow behind the glasses   not suspicion, not yet, but the focused attention of a person whose brain just flagged something without quite identifying it. She's looking at my lashes. I can feel her gaze on them, two points of heat on my eyelids.

<<kira "Hmm.">>

One syllable. Noncommittal. She doesn't push. Kira has this gift   the same gift Marcus has   of letting people keep their mysteries, of filing observations without demanding explanations. She heard my answer. She weighed it. She found it insufficient and she chose to let it go.

For now.

<<kira "Well, it's working. You look good.">>

<<eli "Thanks.">>

<<kira "Like, actually good. Your eyes look... different. Brighter.">>

<<eli "It's the skincare routine.">>

<<kira "What products? I might need to try them.">>

<<eli "Uh. I don't remember the brand. It was at the drugstore.">>

<<kira "You bought skincare products at a drugstore and you don't remember the brand?">>

<<eli "I'm a guy, Kira. We just grab things.">>

She looks at me for one more second. Then she puts the car in drive and pulls away from the curb and the moment passes, and the splinter in my chest pushes a fraction of a millimeter deeper and I think: that was close. That was the first test. There will be more.

[[Arrive at Kira's apartment|day_3_26_kira]]

:: day_3_26_kira
<<set $hint to "Spend time with Kira">>
<<set $locationImage to "images/locations/kira_apartment.jpeg">>
<<set $locationName to "Kira's Apartment">>
Kira's apartment is warm and lit and alive in the way my apartment is cold and dim and dying. Packet   the grey tabby, the keyboard sitter, the hisser   is on the couch when we walk in. He looks at me with the flat, appraising gaze of an animal that knows things humans don't, and I swear he can see through the eyelashes and the brows and the lie to the person underneath.

<<kira "Packet, be nice. Eli's our guest.">>

Packet does not be nice. Packet stares at me for four seconds, then turns his back and begins washing his paw with the elaborate disinterest of a creature that considers me beneath its notice. I respect this. Packet and I have an understanding: I don't expect affection and he doesn't provide any. It's the most honest relationship in this apartment.

<<kira "Tea?">>

<<eli "Sure.">>

She disappears into the kitchen. I hear the kettle fill, the burner click. I stand in the living room   the grey sectional, the coffee table with the manga and the orchid figurine now moved to a small shelf, the framed prints on the walls. Kira's apartment is curated the way her stream is curated   every object chosen, every placement intentional, the physical space reflecting the same aesthetic control she applies to her digital one. It's not obsessive. It's just... considered. The apartment of a person who thinks about where things go and why.

My apartment is the apartment of a person who dropped things and left them where they fell.

She returns with two mugs. Tea   green, the good kind, not the bag-in-a-cup kind. She hands me one and settles onto the couch, drawing her legs up beneath her, and pats the cushion beside her.

<<kira "Sit. I queued up an anime. Have you seen Frieren?">>

<<eli "No.">>

<<kira "Oh my GOD. Eli. You're going to love it. It's about this elf mage who outlives all her friends and then goes on a journey to understand human emotions and it's beautiful and devastating and the pacing is perfect and I cried four times.">>

<<eli "You're really selling the 'I cried four times' angle.">>

<<kira "Crying is a feature, not a bug.">>

I sit beside her. The couch cushions are firm and supportive   everything in Kira's life is firm and supportive, including the couch and the career and the girl herself   and I sink into them the way I sink into her company. Packet, having completed his paw-washing, relocates to the arm of the couch with the resigned tolerance of a cat who's accepted that the couch is no longer exclusively his territory.

Kira starts the show. The screen glows. The opening credits play   soft music, painted backgrounds, the kind of animation that looks like watercolors in motion. She tucks herself against my side with the natural, practiced motion of someone who's been waiting to do this, and I lift my arm and it goes around her shoulders and her head finds the hollow between my shoulder and my chest and she fits there like the space was designed for her.

Her hair is against my jaw. I can smell her shampoo   something floral, different from the cherry blossom of the car, more personal, the scent of her actual head on my actual shoulder. Her glasses press into my collarbone. She adjusts them without looking, one hand reaching up to tilt the frames so they're not digging in, and her fingers brush my neck in the process and the touch sends a ripple down my spine that I absorb without moving.

<<kira "Comfortable?">>

<<eli "Yeah.">>

<<kira "Good. No moving for the next three episodes.">>

<<eli "Three?">>

<<kira "Minimum. Frieren requires commitment.">>

We watch. The anime is   she's right, it's beautiful. A story about time and loss and the slow realization that the things you didn't pay attention to were the things that mattered most. An immortal elf looking back at a life she lived alongside mortals and understanding, too late, that the moments she dismissed as trivial were the only ones worth keeping.

I watch it and I feel things. Not the numb, flat nothing that usually occupies the space behind my sternum during screen time, but actual, genuine things   sadness and warmth and the ache of recognition, the specific ache of watching a character realize they wasted time and knowing you're doing the same thing. Kira shifts against me, adjusting her position, pressing closer. I hold her tighter. Not dramatically   just a fractional increase in the arm around her shoulders, a thumb moving slowly against the fabric of her cardigan. She makes a sound. A small "mmm" of contentment, barely audible, more vibration than voice.

The second episode ends. The third starts. Kira's commentary   which was frequent and passionate during the first episode ("LOOK at that background, Eli, LOOK at it")   has gotten quieter. Her voice is softer. Her observations are shorter. The gaps between them are longer.

Halfway through the third episode, she stops talking entirely.

I look down. Her eyes are closed. Her breathing has changed   slower, deeper, the rhythm of sleep. Her glasses have slipped down her nose. One hand is resting on my chest, the fingers loosely curled, the pixel-heart tattoo visible on her inner wrist. Her lips are slightly parted. There's a strand of hair across her cheek that the breathing moves, gently, back and forth, back and forth.

She's asleep.

Kira Nomura   12,400 followers, 890 average viewers, $487 per session, bisexual, sharp-tongued, wire-framed, the most competent person I've ever met   is asleep on my shoulder at 8:45 PM on a Thursday in January, and I am going to sit here without moving for as long as she sleeps because waking her would be a crime.

I sit perfectly still. I breathe shallowly. The anime plays   the third episode continues, and I watch it over the top of her head, and the story unfolds, and the elf mage learns something about humans that she should have known before, and Kira sleeps against me with the trusting weight of a person who feels safe.

Packet, who has been observing from the arm of the couch, stands, stretches, and walks across the cushions to settle on Kira's lap. He curls into a circle, tucks his nose into his tail, and begins to purr. The vibration transfers through Kira's body into mine. Two living things, warm and sleeping and touching, and me   awake, holding them both, the stillest I've been in months.

I think about the eyelashes. The brows. The wig in my closet. The ElliePlayz dashboard with its $47 and its 51 followers. The RegenaForm receipt I deleted from my email. The lie I told thirty minutes ago in this car   "new skincare routine"   and the way Kira squinted and said "hmm" and let it go.

The splinter.

I can feel it now, deeper than before, positioned between the warmth of her body and the cold calculation of my afternoon. Two realities coexisting in the same chest: the real and the secret. The girl who fell asleep trusting me and the person I'm becoming without telling her. She's on my shoulder and I have her trust and I'm going to go home tonight and put on a wig and sit in front of a webcam and pretend to be a woman for money.

The anime ends. A fourth episode auto-plays. Kira sleeps through it. Packet purrs. I don't move.

I sit there for forty minutes.

[[Wait till Kira wakes up|day_3_27_kira]]

:: day_3_27_kira
<<set $day_step to 27>>
She wakes at 9:30. The waking is slow   a stirring, a shift, a small confused sound as she orients herself. She pushes up from my shoulder, blinks, squints at the screen where a fifth episode has started.

<<kira "Oh my god. How long was I out?">>

<<eli "About forty-five minutes.">>

<<kira "Why didn't you wake me?">>

<<eli "You looked comfortable.">>

<<kira "Eli. You sat still for forty-five minutes?">>

<<eli "My arm is completely numb. I may never use it again.">>

She laughs. Sleepy, rough, the laugh of a person still halfway in a dream. She pushes her glasses up   they slipped almost to the tip of her nose during sleep   and runs a hand through her hair, which is flattened on the side that was against my shoulder, a lopsided bob that makes her look younger and softer and entirely devastating.

<<kira "You're ridiculous.">>

<<eli "You drooled on my shirt.">>

<<kira "I did NOT ">>

She looks at my shoulder. There's a small damp spot. Her face goes bright red.

<<kira "Oh my god. I'm so sorry.">>

<<eli "It's fine. I've had worse things on this shirt.">>

<<kira "That is NOT reassuring.">>

She swats my arm   the dead one, which I can't feel   and then leans in and kisses me. Not the hello kiss from the car. A longer one. A thank-you kiss. A "you sat still for forty-five minutes because I was sleeping" kiss. Her lips are dry from sleep and her breath is tea-warm and she tastes like green tea and mochi residue and the specific chemistry of a person who trusts you enough to fall asleep on your body.

<<kira "Thank you for not moving.">>

<<eli "Anytime.">>

<<kira "I mean it. That's... that's a thing. You know? Letting someone sleep. Not everyone does that.">>

<<eli "Your cat helped. He was on your lap. I didn't want to disturb the hierarchy.">>

<<kira "Packet does outrank you. That's true.">>

She stretches. Her cardigan rides up, showing a strip of her stomach   the black tank top beneath, the soft skin above the waistband of her jeans. I look and look away and she catches me looking and smiles the teasing grin and I feel the warm pulse in my chest that is Kira, that has always been Kira, that exists in the same space as the splinter but is larger, warmer, more real.

<<kira "It's late. I should let you go home. You have streaming stuff tomorrow, right?">>

<<eli "Yeah. Streaming stuff.">>

<<kira "That's all you ever say. 'Streaming stuff.' One day you're going to tell me what actually happens in those streams.">>

<<eli "Death. Mostly. In-game death and DarkSlayer.">>

<<kira "Your biggest fan.">>

<<eli "My biggest bully.">>

<<kira "Same energy.">>

She walks me to the door. Packet watches from the couch with the impassive gaze of a feline sovereign observing the departure of a foreign dignitary. At the door, Kira leans against the frame   the same pose from the photos she posts on socials, casual and composed, the doorframe framing her the way the ring light frames her on stream.

<<kira "Tomorrow?">>

<<eli "Tomorrow.">>

<<kira "I have a stream at noon but I'm free after four. We could do something.">>

<<eli "I'd like that.">>

<<kira "Cool. It's a plan.">>

She reaches up. Her hand   small, warm, the pixel-heart tattoo at the wrist   touches my face. My cheek. The skin below the new lashes and above the new brows, in the space between the two modifications, the space that's still entirely Eli. Her thumb traces my cheekbone the way her thumb traced the condensation on the beer glass, the way her thumb traced circles on the back of my hand at the izakaya. A slow, deliberate circuit.

<<kira "You really do look different.">>

<<eli "Good different?">>

<<kira "Yeah. Good different. Your eyes are... I don't know. Bigger.">>

<<eli "It's the skincare routine.">>

<<kira "Eli.">>

<<eli "What?">>

She looks at me. The squint again. The focused attention. She's not buying it   I can see it in the way her brow furrows (her natural brow, the one that hasn't been sculpted by a clinic four blocks from my apartment), in the way her lips press together the way they do when she's choosing between pushing and letting go.

She lets go.

<<kira "Nothing. Just... you look nice. That's all.">>

<<eli "Thanks, Kira.">>

<<kira "Goodnight, disaster. ❤️">>

<<eli "Goodnight.">>

She kisses me. Goodnight kiss. Soft, brief, certain. Her hand still on my cheek. Then she pulls back and steps inside and the door closes and I stand in the hallway with her fingerprints on my face and her trust in my pocket and the lie between my teeth like a seed I can't swallow and can't spit out.
<<set $hint to "Go Home and Stream">>
[[Go Home|apartment_map]]

:: day_3_28_kira

I get home at 10:15. The apartment. The crack. The hum.

I close the door. Stand in the dark. Don't turn on the light. The January night presses against the window and the building settles and the pipes clank and I stand there in my grey shirt with the drool spot on the shoulder and I feel the two realities pressing against each other inside my chest like tectonic plates.

The warm one: Kira. The couch. The sleeping. The trust. The kiss.

The cold one: ElliePlayz. The dashboard. The $47. The 89 viewers. The clinic. The wig in the closet.

Both real. Both mine. Both impossible to hold simultaneously without something cracking.

I turn on the light. The bulb flickers twice. Everything appears. The mattress, the desk, the monitors, the fridge, the burrito wrappers (two now, a growing collection). The closet with the wig buried in it.

I sit at the desk. Open the closet. Pull the wig out. Set it on the desk beside the monitors.

It sits there   brownish-auburn, soft waves, the StreamLine series from RegenaForm, designed for camera use   and it looks like a prop. A costume piece. A thing that belongs to someone else, waiting to be put on.

I open OBS. Open VoxMorph. Open the ElliePlayz channel. The dashboard from this afternoon's stream is still visible:

Peak Viewers: 89
Average Viewers: 63
Followers: 51
Donations: $47.00

51 followers. Some of them said they'd be back tonight. MidnightWhale99 said "tonight. I'll be there." ChillVibesOnly said "best new streamer on Twitch." People are waiting. Strangers who've never seen my face are waiting for a voice that doesn't exist to come back.

I put on the wig.

The process takes ten minutes   I watched a YouTube tutorial on the walk home, pausing at streetlights, memorizing the steps. Tuck the natural hair. Position the cap. Align the lace front. Secure with adhesive strips. The wig settles onto my head with a weight that's both physical and metaphorical   the feeling of putting on a costume, of stepping into a role, of becoming someone who isn't you and is entirely you.

I look at the webcam preview in OBS. The image is 

Different. Completely different.

The wig changes everything. The brownish-auburn waves frame the face in a way my greasy brown hair never did   soft, warm, feminine. The lashes catch the monitor light and cast those tiny shadows. The brows, now almost fully reshaped, lift the eye area into something open and inviting. The combination   wig, lashes, brows   doesn't make me look female. Not yet. The jaw is still too angular, the nose too wide, the skin too rough. But from certain angles, in certain light, with certain framing 

I adjust the webcam. Tilt it down. Frame the shot from the nose up without nose visibility. eyes, eyelashes, eye brows,forehead and the cascade of wig-hair falling from the top of my head. I angle my desk lamp to the side, creating a warm, directional light that softens the jaw shadow and makes the skin look smoother than it is.

In the preview, from the nose up without nose visibility, in this lighting, with this wig, I look like 

I look like a girl.

Not a beautiful girl. Not a convincing girl. But a plausible one. A girl who's shy about showing her face. A girl who streams with the camera low because she's nervous. A girl named Ellie who has a cute voice and 51 followers and whose full face reveal is always "coming eventually."

I turn on VoxMorph. Route the mic through OBS. Test the voice.

<<chat "ElliePlayz" "Hey everyone, welcome back.">>

The voice comes through the monitoring feed   feminine, warm, the same voice from this afternoon   and combined with the visual of the wig and the chin and the lips in the webcam preview, it's... convincing. Not flawless. Not indistinguishable. But convincing the way a good magic trick is convincing   you know it's not real, but if you don't look too hard, the illusion holds.

I check the time. 11:00 PM. Late-night Twitch. The same time slot that worked this afternoon (which was actually early afternoon, different vibe, but the late-night slot is the one the guide recommended for new streamers).

I change the OBS scene. Add the webcam   the framing without even nose visibility framing, the warm lighting. The ElliePlayz overlay, the pink-purple gradient, the cursive text. The game capture. Chat.

I hover over "Go Live."

[[Go Live|day_3_29_kira]]

:: day_3_29_kira
<<nobr>>
<<set $right_sidebar_mode to 1>>
<<set $playerImage to "images/user/tier_0_3.jpeg">>
<<set $day_step to 29>>
<<set $viewers to 0>>
<<set $followers to 51>>
<</nobr>>

My phone sits on the desk beside me. The last message on it is from Kira: a red heart emoji, sent as I left her apartment. The heart glows in the notification bar, a small red pulse, and I look at it and I look at the "Go Live" button and the distance between them is the distance between the person Kira thinks I am and the person I'm about to be.

The wig is on. The VoxMorph is calibrated and the webcam   this is where the plan lives   the webcam is tilted UP.

Not down. Up. Angled so the frame catches from the nose upward. Eyes. Lashes. Brows. Hair. The feminized zone. Everything RegenaForm touched today, everything the software processes, everything the wig transforms   it's all above the nose and everything that gives me away   the angular jaw, the thin masculine lips, the shadow of the Adam's apple   is below it, cropped out, invisible to anyone watching.

I check the OBS preview. The framing is   it's startling.

In the small rectangle of the webcam feed, a girl looks back at me. Not a whole girl. A suggestion of one. Large brown eyes framed by dense, dark, dramatically long lashes that fan outward and catch the desk lamp light in tiny bright points along their length. Soft feathered brows arched in a gentle feminine curve above the eyes, creating the open, inviting look that Dr. Maren described as "lighter." and the hair   the warm brownish-auburn waves cascading down both sides of the frame, a center part revealing a realistic hairline, the waves catching warm lamp light on the left and cool monitor glow on the right, two-toned and beautiful.

From the nose up, there is nothing masculine in this frame. The eyes could belong to any girl. The lashes are indistinguishable from natural. The brows are delicate and arched. The hair is long and warm and real-looking. The forehead is smooth, the brow ridge softened by the reshaping. A viewer scrolling the browse page would see this thumbnail and their brain would register: female. Pretty. Click.

I angle the desk lamp one more degree to the left. The warm light catches the lashes and they cast those tiny crescent shadows on my cheekbones that I first noticed in Dr. Maren's mirror. The shadows are visible on camera. They read as cheekbone contour. They read as beauty.

I click "Go Live."

[[Go Live|day_3_30_kira]]

